<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Silaharas of North Konkan</h1><p class="st-meta">800 CE – 1265 CE (approx.) · capital Puri</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A coastal dynasty around Thane and Mumbai that ruled the North Konkan for nearly five centuries and built the Ambarnath temple.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silahara-dynasty">Silaharas</a> of North Konkan were established around 800 when the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty</a> appointed Kapardin I to govern the coastal strip around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thane">Thane</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raigad">Raigad</a>. They claimed descent from the legendary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimutavahana">Jimutavahana</a>. Their capital, Puri, is not securely located, and Sthanaka, modern Thane, was another centre.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silahara-dynasty">Silaharas</a> served as feudatories first of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakutas</a> and then of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire</a>. The coast's ports, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanjan">Sanjan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sopara">Sopara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaul">Chaul</a>, were busy with Arab and Persian merchants, and the kings granted privileges to foreign traders, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parsis">Parsi</a> and Muslim communities. Their many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-plate grants are among the main sources for coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>. King Mummuni completed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambarnath">Ambarnath</a> in 1060.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The dynasty fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kadambas-of-goa">Kadambas of Goa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuna-yadava-dynasty">Yadavas</a>. Around 1265 the Yadava king Mahadeva conquered the North Konkan, killing the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silahara-dynasty">Silahara</a> ruler, Someshvara.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 800 CE – 1265 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:75.62%;width:9.25%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 CE – 1265 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Puri</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kapardin I, Arikesarin, Mummuni</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shaivism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit, Marathi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Silaharas of Thane</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1032&amp;focus=silaharas-of-north-konkan" aria-label="Silaharas of North Konkan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6996 1444 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Silaharas of North Konkan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7316 1700L7097 1795M7316 1700L7060 1881"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7097 1795h0M7060 1881h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7316 1700h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7316 1700h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Puri. <a href="/atlas?year=1032&amp;focus=silaharas-of-